1
00:00:04,688 --> 00:00:07,363
When someone is in danger
in the United States,

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,838
there's one number almost everyone knows.

3
00:00:11,025 --> 00:00:14,175
But why is it 911? Who created it?

4
00:00:14,425 --> 00:00:17,750
What did Americans do before 911 existed?

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,550
And perhaps most interestingly…

6
00:00:21,763 --> 00:00:25,700
Was the emergency number actually named
because of the terrorist attacks of

7
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:26,950
September 11?

8
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:34,863
The answer is no. In fact, 911 existed
for more than three decades before 9/11.

9
00:00:36,025 --> 00:00:39,725
The story of 911 begins in the 1960s.

10
00:00:40,925 --> 00:00:45,538
Back then, there was no single emergency
number covering the United States.

11
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,950
If you needed the police,
fire department, or an ambulance,

12
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:52,613
you generally had to know and dial the
local telephone number for the appropriate

13
00:00:52,613 --> 00:00:55,750
service. And that created
a serious problem.

14
00:00:56,925 --> 00:00:59,625
Imagine witnessing a serious accident.

15
00:00:59,838 --> 00:01:02,425
Someone is injured. There's a fire.

16
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:05,475
And you have to stop and figure
out which number to call.

17
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:07,175
Every second matters.

18
00:01:08,313 --> 00:01:12,125
The idea of a single emergency
number wasn't completely new.

19
00:01:12,375 --> 00:01:18,463
In fact, Britain had already introduced
999 as an emergency number in 1937.

20
00:01:19,638 --> 00:01:24,625
In the United States, the idea gained
momentum during the 1950s and 1960s.

21
00:01:24,625 --> 00:01:30,513
In 1967, the President's Commission on Law
Enforcement and Administration of Justice

22
00:01:30,513 --> 00:01:34,363
recommended creating a single
number for reporting emergencies.

23
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:38,875
The Federal Communications Commission then
worked with AT&T to find a number that

24
00:01:38,875 --> 00:01:40,963
could be implemented across the country.

25
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:44,188
So why did they choose 911?

26
00:01:44,438 --> 00:01:48,538
AT&T selected the number because
it was short, easy to remember,

27
00:01:50,875 --> 00:01:54,713
and—critically—had not already been
assigned as an area code, office code,

28
00:01:55,113 --> 00:01:57,263
or service code in the telephone system.

29
00:01:58,438 --> 00:02:01,063
It also fit the technology of the time.

30
00:02:01,313 --> 00:02:04,075
Remember, these were the
days of rotary phones.

31
00:02:04,313 --> 00:02:08,325
The number needed to be simple enough to
remember and quick enough to dial during

32
00:02:08,325 --> 00:02:09,325
an emergency.

33
00:02:10,425 --> 00:02:16,038
Before 911, one common option was to
dial 0 and reach a telephone operator.

34
00:02:16,275 --> 00:02:19,975
The operator could then help connect
the caller to the appropriate emergency

35
00:02:19,975 --> 00:02:20,975
service.

36
00:02:21,925 --> 00:02:24,888
But this wasn't a
dedicated emergency system.

37
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:28,388
It depended on the operator,
local telephone infrastructure,

38
00:02:28,563 --> 00:02:31,663
and knowing where the appropriate
emergency service was.

39
00:02:32,838 --> 00:02:37,363
As emergency calls increased and society
became more dependent on telephones,

40
00:02:37,550 --> 00:02:40,275
the limitations became
increasingly obvious.

41
00:02:40,525 --> 00:02:44,675
Fire officials had already been pushing
for a simpler emergency number.

42
00:02:44,675 --> 00:02:46,425
In 1957,

43
00:02:46,563 --> 00:02:51,075
the National Association of Fire Chiefs
reportedly recommended a single number for

44
00:02:51,075 --> 00:02:52,250
reporting fires.

45
00:02:53,450 --> 00:02:54,850
The goal was simple:

46
00:02:59,638 --> 00:03:02,988
Then came February 16th, 1968.

47
00:03:03,238 --> 00:03:04,975
In Haleyville, Alabama,

48
00:03:05,225 --> 00:03:09,400
something happened that would eventually
change emergency communications across

49
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:10,400
America.

50
00:03:13,488 --> 00:03:18,488
Alabama Speaker of the House Rankin Fite
picked up a telephone and dialed 911.

51
00:03:18,738 --> 00:03:20,388
The call was answered by U.S.

52
00:03:20,388 --> 00:03:23,338
Representative Tom Bevill
at the local police station.

53
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:27,388
It was the first official 911
call in the United States.

54
00:03:28,638 --> 00:03:30,038
But here's something important:

55
00:03:30,575 --> 00:03:34,575
911 didn't instantly become
available everywhere in America.

56
00:03:34,813 --> 00:03:36,950
The system took years to expand.

57
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:41,263
Different communities and telephone
companies had to build the infrastructure

58
00:03:41,263 --> 00:03:43,525
necessary to route emergency calls.

59
00:03:43,775 --> 00:03:47,875
Eventually, 911 became the
standard Americans know today.

60
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,225
So what actually prompted
the creation of 911?

61
00:03:52,462 --> 00:03:54,475
There wasn't one single event.

62
00:03:54,688 --> 00:03:58,725
It was the growing realization that
emergency communication needed to become

63
00:03:58,725 --> 00:04:01,688
faster, simpler, and more reliable.

64
00:04:02,925 --> 00:04:06,500
The United States was experiencing
growing numbers of emergencies,

65
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:10,075
including serious injuries
from automobile crashes.

66
00:04:10,325 --> 00:04:15,638
A landmark 1966 report from the National
Academy of Sciences highlighted accidental

67
00:04:15,638 --> 00:04:20,200
death and injury as a major public-health
problem and called for exploring a single

68
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:23,088
nationwide telephone number
to summon an ambulance.

69
00:04:24,237 --> 00:04:25,275
At the same time,

70
00:04:25,612 --> 00:04:29,862
police and fire officials were pushing
for simpler ways for the public to report

71
00:04:29,862 --> 00:04:32,475
emergencies. In other words…

72
00:04:35,825 --> 00:04:37,737
Now let's cross the Atlantic.

73
00:04:37,975 --> 00:04:40,475
The United States uses 911.

74
00:04:40,713 --> 00:04:43,325
The United Kingdom uses 999.

75
00:04:43,538 --> 00:04:45,750
So are they basically the same thing?

76
00:04:45,963 --> 00:04:47,263
In purpose, yes.

77
00:04:47,513 --> 00:04:51,938
Both are emergency numbers designed to
connect people with emergency services.

78
00:04:52,175 --> 00:04:56,350
But they are different numbers belonging
to different national telephone systems.

79
00:04:57,537 --> 00:04:59,475
And here's another interesting fact:

80
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:04,325
The UK also supports 112, the
pan-European emergency number.

81
00:05:04,550 --> 00:05:07,763
So if you're in Britain,
you don't normally dial 911.

82
00:05:08,013 --> 00:05:11,550
You dial 999 or 112 for emergencies.

83
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:17,612
So if an American tourist in London
instinctively dials 911… That's not the

84
00:05:17,612 --> 00:05:19,112
number they should rely on.

85
00:05:20,338 --> 00:05:24,338
Now for one of the biggest
misconceptions surrounding 911.

86
00:05:24,588 --> 00:05:28,916
Some people assume the emergency number
was created because of the September

87
00:05:28,916 --> 00:05:30,275
11 terrorist attacks.

88
00:05:30,513 --> 00:05:31,862
But look at the dates.

89
00:05:33,025 --> 00:05:36,625
The first 911 call happened in 1968.

90
00:05:36,863 --> 00:05:40,200
The September 11 attacks happened in 2001.

91
00:05:40,438 --> 00:05:42,700
That's a difference of 33 years.

92
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:47,975
So there is no causal connection between
the terrorist attacks and the creation of

93
00:05:47,975 --> 00:05:50,038
the 911 emergency number.

94
00:05:50,275 --> 00:05:52,963
The similarity is simply a coincidence.

95
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:57,838
So, is 911 only used in the United States?

96
00:05:58,050 --> 00:06:02,963
No. A number of countries and territories
use 911 as an emergency number,

97
00:06:03,550 --> 00:06:07,300
either as their primary emergency
number or alongside other numbers.

98
00:06:07,550 --> 00:06:12,113
Examples include Canada, Mexico,
the Philippines, American Samoa,

99
00:06:12,363 --> 00:06:15,787
Antigua and Barbuda, and
several Caribbean countries.

100
00:06:17,037 --> 00:06:18,487
But there's an important warning:

101
00:06:18,925 --> 00:06:23,125
Don't assume that 911 works everywhere
just because it works in the United

102
00:06:23,125 --> 00:06:27,487
States. Emergency numbers vary
dramatically around the world.

103
00:06:28,713 --> 00:06:32,062
And some countries support
multiple emergency numbers.

104
00:06:32,313 --> 00:06:36,525
For example, the United
Kingdom uses 999 and 112.

105
00:06:36,775 --> 00:06:41,150
Here are some important emergency numbers
you may encounter around the world.

106
00:06:47,737 --> 00:06:51,888
And this is why travelers should check the
local emergency number before visiting

107
00:06:51,888 --> 00:06:52,888
another country.

108
00:06:53,075 --> 00:06:57,213
The number that saves a life in one
country might do absolutely nothing in

109
00:06:57,213 --> 00:06:58,213
another.

110
00:06:59,025 --> 00:07:03,600
One of the biggest advantages of systems
like 911 is that you don't necessarily

111
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:05,987
have to know which
emergency service you need.

112
00:07:06,225 --> 00:07:08,050
You explain what is happening.

113
00:07:08,287 --> 00:07:12,450
The call-taker can determine what kind
of response is required and dispatch the

114
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:14,300
appropriate emergency services.

115
00:07:15,525 --> 00:07:18,875
That's the real power of a
universal emergency number.

116
00:07:19,088 --> 00:07:21,713
It's not just about
remembering three digits.

117
00:07:21,925 --> 00:07:26,175
It's about removing a decision from
someone who may already be panicking.

118
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:28,487
So let's recap.

119
00:07:47,537 --> 00:07:50,125
Three digits. Easy to remember.

120
00:07:50,375 --> 00:07:54,000
Easy to dial. And in the right situation…

121
00:07:57,812 --> 00:08:02,925
…those three digits can be the difference
between waiting for help… and getting it.
